WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.660
Secure storage decisions define who can read key material and how much evidence exists when something changes.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:14.920
Keys should live outside source code, outside build artifacts, and outside casual configuration paths whenever possible.

3
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:30.080
Managed secret stores, key-management systems, hardware-backed storage, platform keychains, HSM-style services, encrypted configuration, restricted files, and deployment-time secret injection all represent different tradeoffs.

4
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:35.140
The right choice depends on the platform, policy, and operational model.

5
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:40.620
Hardcoded secrets and private keys in repositories are high-priority findings.

6
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:47.280
They spread through commit history, backups, forks, build logs, and developer machines.

7
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.820
Removing the line later does not erase all copies.

8
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.500
Plaintext configuration files and broad environment variables also deserve review.

9
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.600
They may be visible to operators, child processes, crash collection, diagnostics, or deployment tooling.

10
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.460
Treat them as exposure-sensitive rather than automatically safe.

11
00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:09.180
Storage design should answer practical questions.

12
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.440
Who or what can read the key?

13
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.220
Is access audited?

14
00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.720
Can the key rotate without redeploying everything?

15
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.700
Can old data still be read during migration?

16
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.840
How does the application fail if the backend is unavailable?

17
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.360
Local copies should be minimized.

18
00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:35.820
Developer convenience exports, shared test files, copied production configuration, and temporary debugging material can outlive their original purpose and create avoidable risk.

19
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:45.620
A good backend does not remove the need for discipline, but it gives teams a controlled place to enforce access, auditing, rotation, and operational ownership.

20
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:53.500
C++ services should also avoid spreading retrieved secrets through process-wide globals unless policy explicitly supports that pattern.

21
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.640
A narrow retrieval path makes it easier to audit who used the key and why.

22
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:08.520
When local caching is required for reliability or performance, define cache lifetime, refresh behavior, revocation handling, and cleanup expectations before deployment.
